Body:
Thanks @cpu150, that bisection in #50236 is very helpful.

Regarding the `SchemaError`: in our testing, this unhandled rejection consistently appears in stderr during the initial boot sequence when starting ACP with `--log-level all --print-logs`.

Here is a minimal standalone Node probe reproducing the log output:

```js
const { spawn } = require("child_process");

const cp = spawn("opencode", ["acp", "--log-level", "all", "--print-logs"], {
  cwd: process.cwd(),
  stdio: ["pipe", "pipe", "pipe"]
});

cp.stderr.on("data", (d) => process.stderr.write(d));

cp.stdout.on("data", (chunk) => {
  for (const line of chunk.toString().split("\n")) {
    if (!line.trim()) continue;
    const msg = JSON.parse(line);
    if (msg.id === 1) {
      cp.stdin.write(JSON.stringify({
        jsonrpc: "2.0",
        id: 2,
        method: "session/new",
        params: { cwd: process.cwd(), mcpServers: [] }
      }) + "\n");
    } else if (msg.id === 2) {
      setTimeout(() => { cp.kill(); process.exit(0); }, 1000);
    }
  }
});

cp.stdin.write(JSON.stringify({
  jsonrpc: "2.0",
  id: 1,
  method: "initialize",
  params: { protocolVersion: 1, clientCapabilities: {} }
}) + "\n");
```

On 2.0.6 and 2.0.7, right after the server broadcasts `event.type=command.updated`, the CLI process logs:

```text
level=ERROR message="unhandled rejection" cause="SchemaError(Missing key at [\"path\"])" role=cli
```

It looks like two compounding problems:
1. As you noted in #50236, `session/new` races ahead before config providers activate.
2. When the background server attempts to broadcast subsequent updates over the event stream to catch up, the CLI subscriber crashes deserialising the event schema, so the catalog never receives the late-arriving models.

I'll investigate this issue and try to reproduce it locally.

I can set up a minimal reproduction with three independent Task calls and measure whether the subagent sessions overlap. If I find the path causing the sequential run, I'll fix it and add a regression test.

@neriousy, are you already working on this? If not, I'll take a look.

@weiguangli-io showing interest here.

i see no reason why this feature shouldn't be implemented, and a lot of reasons why it should. i mean, why should sub-agents lose all of their work on session interruptions? and what's the reasoning behind keeping this behavior?

to me it seems like a purely QoL feature that would prevent having to re-start interrupted sub-agents when they could instead be resumed.

